  "strategy": "### 1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe proposed approach uses gradient-based optimization with automatic differentiation to iteratively refine the step function h. This method leverages the differentiable nature of the overlap integral objective while enforcing constraints through projection and normalization.\n\nThe key innovations are:\n1. **Automatic Differentiation**: Uses TensorFlow for efficient gradient computation, enabling precise optimization in high dimensions.\n2. **Constraint Handling**: Maintains feasibility through clipping and normalization while allowing continuous updates.\n3. **Grid Adaptation**: Starts with a coarse grid and adaptively refines it during optimization.\n\nThis approach directly targets the non-convex objective by:\n- Using the initial_h_values as a warm start\n- Applying adaptive learning rates\n- Projecting to feasible solutions after each update\n\nLimitations:\n- Local minima possible due to non-convexity\n- Requires careful learning rate scheduling\n- Clipping can introduce discontinuities in updates\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification\n\nLet \\( h \\in [0, 1]^{n_{\\text{points}}} \\) represent the step function samples.\n\n#### Objective\nMinimize:\n\\[\nC_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1 - h, \\text{mode=\"full\"}) \\cdot \\Delta x \\right)\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\Delta x = 2 / n_{\\text{points}} \\)\n\n#### Constraints\n1. \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) for all \\( i \\)\n2. \\( \\sum h[i] \\cdot \\Delta x = 1 \\)\n\n#### Differentiable Approximation\nThe cross-correlation is differentiable in \\( h \\) except at boundaries. We use the TensorFlow implementation for gradients.\n\n### 3. Implementation Procedure\n\n#### 1. Initialization\n- If `initial_h_values` is provided:\n  - Use as initial guess, normalized to meet the integral constraint.\n  - Convert to TensorFlow Variable for differentiable operations.\n- Else:\n  - Initialize with uniform random samples in [0, 1], normalized.\n\n#### 2. Optimization Loop\nFor each iteration:\n1. Compute the overlap integral using TensorFlow's `signal.correlate`.\n2. Calculate the maximum overlap \\( C_5 \\).\n3. Compute gradients \\( \\nabla_h C_5 \\) using TensorFlow's automatic differentiation.\n4. Update \\( h \\) using Adam optimizer with adaptive learning rates:\n   \\[\n   h = h - \\alpha \\cdot \\nabla_h C_5\n   \\]\n5. Project \\( h \\) to [0, 1] via clipping.\n6. Normalize \\( h \\) to satisfy the integral constraint:\n   \\[\n   h = \\frac{h}{\\sum h \\cdot \\Delta x} \\cdot \\Delta x\n   \\]\n7. Track the best solution (lowest \\( C_5 \\)).\n\n#### 3. Grid Adaptation\n- Start with \\( n_{\\text{points}} = 70 \\)\n- After 500 iterations, double \\( n_{\\text{points}} \\) and interpolate \\( h \\)\n- Repeat until \\( n_{\\text{points}} = 512 \\)\n\n#### 4. Termination\n- Stop when time budget exhausted or no improvement for 1000 iterations\n- Return the best valid candidate found\n\n### 4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n\n#### Default Configuration\n- Initial learning rate: \\( \\alpha = 0.01 \\)\n- Adam optimizer parameters: \\( \\beta_1 = 0.9, \\beta_2 = 0.999 \\)\n- Iterations per grid level: 1000\n- Refinement steps: 3 (70 \u2192 140 \u2192 280 \u2192 560 points)\n\n#### Variations\n1. **Learning Rate Schedules**: Exponential decay, step decay\n2. **Different Initializations**: Random with different distributions\n3. **Grid Refinement Frequency**: More frequent vs less frequent\n\n### 5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n#### Time Estimates\n- Each iteration: ~1ms (vectorized TensorFlow operations)\n- 1000 iterations per grid level: ~1s\n- Total with 4 grid levels: ~400s\n\n#### Robustness\n- Clip and normalize after each update\n- Track best solution separately\n- Handle infeasible solutions by projection\n\n### 6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n#### Feasibility Checks\n- Ensure \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) via clipping\n- Verify integral constraint via normalization\n\n#### Final Evaluation\n- Compute \\( C_5 \\) with evaluator\n- Return the best h, its \\( C_5 \\), and \\( n_{\\text{points}} \\)\n- Always return a valid solution, even if initial_h_values is used",
